    "result": "I've successfully fixed the bug in the dates module where invoices with 'eom' (end of month) payment terms issued in February of a leap year were getting the wrong due date.\n\n## Problem Summary\nThe `end_of_month` function in `invoicing/dates.py` was using a hardcoded array that always treated February as having 28 days, regardless of whether the year was a leap year. This caused February 29, 2024 to be calculated as February 28, 2024.\n\n## Solution Implemented\nI modified the `end_of_month` function to:\n1. Check if the month is February \n2. For February, use Python's `calendar.monthrange()` which correctly handles leap years\n3. For other months, maintain the existing logic for backward compatibility\n\n## Verification Results\n- \u2705 The originally failing tests now pass:\n  - `test_end_of_month_leap_february()`\n  - `test_eom_terms_in_leap_year()`\n- \u2705 All 85 tests in the test suite pass\n- \u2705 Edge cases work correctly:\n  - Leap year February (2024) \u2192 29 days\n  - Non-leap year February (2023) \u2192 28 days\n  - Other months work as before\n\nThe fix is general, robust, and maintains full backward compatibility while correctly handling leap years for all February dates.",
